in 2007 Joel and Ethan Coen released No  Country for Old Men it quickly became  established as one of their greatest  films but it's ambiguous final scene  confuses audiences to this day so let's  journey back to Texas in 1980 and delve  into the ending of No Country for Old  Men the primary narrative of the film  has kicked off when the wellin moss  played by Josh Brolin stumbles upon the  aftermath of a shootout and walks away  with a satchel packed full of money the  deadly confrontation involved two major  crime organizations one based in Mexico  and the other in the United States  viewers know the Mexican half of this  drug deal went seriously wrong but it's  clear from the events of the film that  the other side may be worse off by the  time that dust settles the American  organization attempting to buy the drug  says neither the goods they wanted nor  the cash that Llewellyn made off with  then to make matters worse Javier Bardem  s character Anton Chigurh launches a  reign of terror against them that seems  to go all the way to the top and as  anyone who's seen this movie knows you  do not want to be on the wrong side of  Anton Chigurh it's just supposed to be  the old badass thanks to the relentless  sugar No Country for Old Men is a  white-knuckle ride with shootouts in the  street life-or-death decisions based on  the toss of a coin and harrowing scenes  involving a captive bolt pistol that was  meant to be used in slaughterhouses  however along with action and suspense  the film has plenty of introspective  moments and thoughtful themes that give  viewers a lot to think about after the  movie is over much of No Country for Old  Men Paulo sheriff edy Tom Bell played by  Tommy Lee Jones as he becomes  increasingly concerned about his place  in the world as an aging lawman the  deeper Bell gets into the situation  involving moss sugar and the missing  money the more concerned he is about the  future at one point he flat-out says  that he feels overmatched and worries  that the violence around him is  escalating in a way that he can't  contend with by the end of the film Bell  is retired with the sense of law and  order upended by the moss affair which  means someone else has to follow in his  footsteps and become the next Sheriff  whether that's his deputy or someone  else Bell will no doubt have some  standing in his community as a kind of  elder statesman the MacGuffin at the  center of No Country for Old Men is a  case containing more than two million  dollars in cash we watch as the money  changes hands from the drug dealers to  moss and then to the hit man sugar so  what happens to the money after that we  know that a tracking device was placed  in the case from the very beginning to  keep tabs on its whereabouts but Moss  eventually gets rid of  we also know that sugar was still able  to hunt moss down without the tracker  and sugar didn't exactly disappear  quietly even after a massive car  accident and a broken arm he was able to  patch himself up on the spot and simply  walk away will his surviving former  employers track him down or will sugar  make himself disappear if anyone can  manage to hide himself and two million  dollars in plain sight its Anton Chigurh  midway through the film a man in a  mysterious office building hires Woody  Harrelson's character Carson Wells to  seek out boss and get the money back  before sugar gets his hands on it Wells  a savvy contract killer in his own right  is familiar enough with sugar to know  how dangerous he is and smart enough to  try to get the money without directly  involving him unfortunately for Wells  sugar finds him anyway and kills him on  principle if the organization that hired  Wells is still trying to track down  sugar and the money after the film ends  it's easy to imagine that they'll be  sending out other hired killers to see  who can finally bag sugar it's also easy  to imagine him killing every single one  of them without a moment of hesitation  or concern near the end of the film  after he's found lost dead and realized  that the money is gone sheriff Bell  returns home to visit his uncle Ellis a  former lawman living all alone in a  ramshackle house over the course of  their conversation Bell makes it clear  that he feels he can't contend with the  modern class of criminal Ellis responds  with a certain degree of sympathy but  also tells him about a horrific crime  from many decades earlier  well not quite on par with the massive  drug shootout at the beginning of the  film it reminds Belle that violence is  nothing new the world is a dark place  particularly if you devote your life to  fighting on the side of the light Ellis  explains to Belle that it's vanity to  believe that this struggle is new and  unique to him came to uplift commit  ain't no wait no new Bell seems to  concede this point but also feels the  time is right to hang it up the scene is  a perfect encapsulation of the film's  meditation on how violence preys upon  the soul no country for old man is  remembered for a great many things but  perhaps one of the most discussed  moments of the film is its ambiguous  final scene Bell sits at his dining room  table and tells his wife about a dream  he had in the dream he and his father  are riding on horseback through the  night and his father rides past him  silently carrying fire in a horn he  knows his father will be waiting up  ahead having made a fire for them as  bell says in all that dark and all that  cold  the former Sheriff concludes by saying  this he'd be no cook and with that line  both the dream in the film ends leaving  most viewers scratching their heads in  the context of the rest of the movie as  Bell reckons with his place in the world  the dream feels like the conclusion of a  man who knows that death is the next  milestone he's retired from law  enforcement like his late father before  him and so his subconscious is telling  him that the reunion in the afterlife is  imminent but of course spells dream ends  before he joins his father at the fire  which means that he still has some  living left to do check out one of our  newest videos right here plus even more  looper videos about your favorite movies  are coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the bells you don't miss  a single one  [Music]
 

